1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,528
When a payment is slow or stuck, how would anyone know?

2
00:00:03,578 --> 00:00:09,696
One payment crosses the app, an adapter and a scheme, and the logs on each machine can't follow it.

3
00:00:09,746 --> 00:00:17,256
So the app and both adapters import one platform library, observability, and adding the jar is all a service does.

4
00:00:17,306 --> 00:00:27,384
It traces every request with Micrometer Tracing and OpenTelemetry, and carries the trace on every call: to the adapters, through Kafka, and into Temporal.

5
00:00:27,434 --> 00:00:34,368
One trace follows a payment from the payment page, through the app's modules, into the Uzcard adapter and out to the scheme.

6
00:00:34,418 --> 00:00:42,744
Every response names its trace in an X-Request-Id header, a 401 or 403 too, so a caller can quote it to support.

7
00:00:42,794 --> 00:00:48,312
The logs carry the same trace id, so a warning links straight to the trace it belongs to.

8
00:00:48,362 --> 00:00:59,112
Every log line is masked on every sink, every outgoing call too: card numbers keep their first six and last four digits, tokens their last four, and secrets are hidden.

9
00:00:59,162 --> 00:01:08,232
Spring Boot exports JVM, HTTP and database pool metrics, and the app adds only what Boot can't know: how payments end.

10
00:01:08,282 --> 00:01:15,816
Every debit is counted by card scheme and outcome: approved, declined, unknown, or failed before any money moved.

11
00:01:15,866 --> 00:01:21,480
And the scheme's answer time is measured, with fixed buckets that reach past its forty second limit.

12
00:01:21,530 --> 00:01:29,592
Health indicators check the file store, Vault, Keycloak and both adapters, and each one is also a gauge: one while it is up.

13
00:01:29,642 --> 00:01:38,184
Everything goes over OTLP to one stack: Prometheus for metrics, Tempo for traces, Loki for logs, and Grafana on top.

14
00:01:38,234 --> 00:01:46,920
The payments dashboard puts it together: debits by outcome, unknown debits in red, answer times, errors per service, and pool waits.

15
00:01:46,970 --> 00:01:55,704
Grafana's alert rules live as code in the infrastructure repository, and every alert is an email to the on-call list, Mailpit locally.

16
00:01:55,754 --> 00:02:04,560
When the scheme goes silent, the debit shows up as unknown, the adapter's client error fires an alert email, and the warning links to its trace.

17
00:02:04,610 --> 00:02:08,712
Next, what to do when the processing center goes completely silent.
